<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Torpor requests no entitlements at all.

         This dict is deliberately empty, and that emptiness is the point of the
         file: the hardened runtime's defaults are the whole of what Torpor asks
         for, so there is no capability here to widen by accident.

         com.apple.security.automation.apple-events — REMOVED, and worth reading
         before anyone adds it back. Torpor used to send Apple events to Terminal
         and iTerm, to run a hibernated session's resume command in the tab it
         had died in. That is gone: restoring a session is now one action —
         copying its command to the clipboard — and nothing in the app sends an
         Apple event to another process. See SessionControl for the whole closed
         search space behind that decision (TIOCSTI is EPERM, the `code` CLI has
         no exec flag, vscode:// reaches extensions only, and synthetic
         keystrokes would need Accessibility).

         Dropping it is a real reduction in what Torpor can do: macOS will now
         never show this app an Automation consent dialog, and could not grant it
         control of another application even if the user tried. An app that reads
         other processes' memory and sends them signals should not also be able
         to drive Terminal.

         Notifier still calls NSAppleScript, for `display notification` when
         UNUserNotificationCenter is unavailable (an unbundled `swift run`
         build). That needs neither this entitlement nor
         NSAppleEventsUsageDescription, because it is not an inter-process event.
         Measured rather than assumed: an ad-hoc-signed .app with the hardened
         runtime and an empty entitlements set, launched as its own responsible
         process, ran `display notification` successfully and was refused
         `tell application "Finder"` with -1743, "Not authorized to send Apple
         events to Finder". So the notification path survives an empty file and
         the automation path is genuinely closed.

         Also deliberately absent, recorded so nobody adds them speculatively:

         com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
             Sparkle only needs this when the framework is signed by a different
             team than the host. Everything here is signed with one identity in
             one pass, so requiring it would mean something is wrong.

         com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
         com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
             No interpreter, no JIT.

         keychain-access-groups
             Generic passwords in the user's login keychain need no entitlement.
             Torpor reads its own item and, on request, Claude Code's; macOS
             prompts for the second, which is the intended behaviour.

         App Sandbox
             Torpor's whole job is reading other processes' memory via
             proc_pid_rusage and signalling them. The sandbox forbids both, so
             this is a Developer ID app and not a Mac App Store one. -->
</dict>
</plist>
